MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m17",
  "summary": "Explain how automatic transmissions, differentials and four-wheel-drive systems work, check fluids and basic condition, and recognise common faults and the limits of workshop repair.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe the main parts of an automatic transmission and compare it with a CVT and a dual-clutch gearbox.","Explain the torque converter, its lock-up clutch and why the correct fluid matters, and check the fluid level and condition.","Explain how a planetary gear set gives different gear ratios and how clutches, bands and solenoids select them.","Describe the open differential, limited-slip and locking types, and the crown wheel and pinion.","Describe four-wheel and all-wheel-drive systems, low range, driveline wind-up and tyre matching.","Diagnose slipping, harsh shifts, delayed engagement and overheating, and know what a workshop can repair."],
  "cheatsheet": ["A torque converter replaces the clutch. It uses fluid to transmit power and can multiply torque at low speed.","Lock-up clutch: locks the converter at cruising speed to stop slip and save fuel.","Always use the transmission fluid specified in the manual. Fluids are not interchangeable.","ATF is normally red or pink. Dark brown fluid with a burnt smell shows overheating and wear.","Planetary gears: sun, planet carrier with planet gears, and a ring gear. Holding or driving different members gives each ratio.","An open differential lets the wheels turn at different speeds in corners but sends power to the wheel with least grip.","Limited-slip and locking differentials reduce the loss of traction when one wheel slips.","Part-time 4WD on hard dry roads causes driveline wind-up. Use it only on loose or slippery surfaces.","Fit tyres of the same size and wear on all four wheels of an all-wheel-drive vehicle."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m17-l1", "title": "Automatic transmission overview", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An automatic transmission changes gear for the driver. It has a fluid coupling instead of a clutch pedal, and it is controlled by hydraulics and electronics. This lesson gives an overview before the next lessons go into detail."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Torque converter:** a fluid coupling between the engine and the gearbox. It lets the engine keep turning when the vehicle is stopped.","**Planetary gear sets:** sets of gears that produce the different ratios.","**Clutches and bands:** hold or drive parts of the planetary gear sets to select a gear.","**Hydraulic system:** a pump driven by the engine produces oil pressure. A **valve body** with passages and valves directs the pressure to the clutches and bands.","**Electronic control:** a transmission control module (TCM) or the engine computer reads the vehicle speed, throttle and other data and operates **solenoids** in the valve body to shift."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gear selection and park"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The driver chooses **P** (park), **R** (reverse), **N** (neutral) or **D** (drive), and sometimes lower ranges or a manual mode. In park, a **park pawl** locks the output shaft so the vehicle can not roll. The engine can only be started in P or N, through a neutral safety switch (range switch)."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other types in outline"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Notes"],"rows":[["Conventional automatic","Torque converter and planetary gears","Smooth, widely used, fixed number of gears"],["CVT (continuously variable)","A steel belt or chain runs between two pulleys of variable width","No fixed gears. Smooth, but needs the correct fluid and care"],["Dual-clutch (DCT)","Two clutches, one for odd and one for even gears, operated by actuators","Fast shifts, may be wet or dry clutch types"]],"caption":"Types of automatic transmission"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why fluid and condition matter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Automatic transmission fluid (ATF) transmits hydraulic force, lubricates, cools and gives the clutches the correct friction. When it is dirty or overheated, clutches slip, valves stick and the gearbox fails."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Selector positions and driving modes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Position","What it does"],"rows":[["P (park)","Locks the output shaft with a pawl. Engine can be started. Use with the handbrake on slopes."],["R (reverse)","Engages reverse gear. A warning sound or lamp operates on the rear lamps and camera."],["N (neutral)","Disconnects drive. Engine can be started. Used for towing short distances only as the maker allows."],["D (drive)","Automatic selection of all forward gears"],["L, 1, 2 or manual mode","Holds a low gear or lets the driver select gears, useful for engine braking on a descent"],["S or sport","Changes shift points to hold gears for longer"],["Snow or winter","Starts in a higher gear and softens the throttle to reduce wheelspin"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern selectors are electronic: a lever, dial or buttons that send a signal to the gearbox computer. If the battery is flat, an electronic selector may not release park, and there is often a manual release, described in the manual. Do not force a selector."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Most automatic gearboxes fail because of heat and worn fluid. Regular, correct fluid service is the cheapest way of avoiding a very expensive repair."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Ask the customer how the vehicle behaves when cold and hot, and whether they tow or drive in heavy traffic. These details help you to read the symptoms."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l2", "title": "Torque converter and fluid", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The torque converter is the link between the engine and the automatic gearbox. This lesson explains how it works and how to check the fluid."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"torque-converter"},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a torque converter works"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Impeller (pump):** attached to the converter housing, which is bolted to the engine. It spins with the engine and throws the fluid outwards.","**Turbine:** attached to the gearbox input shaft. The fluid hits its blades and makes it turn.","**Stator:** sits between them on a one-way clutch. At low speed, it redirects the returning fluid so it helps to push the impeller, which multiplies the torque. At higher speed, it freewheels.","**Lock-up clutch:** a friction clutch that locks the turbine to the housing at cruising speed. It removes slip, which saves fuel and reduces heat."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"At rest in drive, the engine turns the impeller, but the turbine is held by the brakes. The slip is high and the vehicle does not move much, or creeps slowly. As the throttle opens, the fluid force on the turbine grows and the vehicle moves off. The maximum speed the engine reaches with the vehicle held and the throttle fully open is the **stall speed**. A stall test is a diagnostic test and must be done only as the manual describes, because it generates heat."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fluid types"},
      {"t":"p","text":"There are many ATF specifications, and they are **not interchangeable**. Different fluids have different friction properties for different clutches. Some gearboxes use a special fluid that is not sold as ATF. Using the wrong fluid causes shuddering, slipping and damage. Always check the specification in the manual or on the dipstick or a label."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking level and condition"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Follow the manual. Many gearboxes with a dipstick are checked with the engine running at a set temperature, on level ground, in park or neutral, with the lever moved through the gears first.","Pull the dipstick, wipe it, push it fully home and read the level between the marks.","Look at the colour. New ATF is usually red or pink. Brown or black is old or overheated.","Smell it. A burnt smell shows overheating and worn clutches.","Some modern gearboxes are sealed and have no dipstick. They have a check plug that is opened at a specified temperature, with a scan tool to read it."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fluid condition","Meaning"],"rows":[["Bright red or pink, clean","Good"],["Dark brown, burnt smell","Overheated and worn clutch material"],["Milky or pink and foamy","Water or coolant contamination, often from a cooler fault"],["Metal particles or paste","Internal wear"]],"caption":"Reading ATF"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stall speed and the stall test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **stall speed** is the highest speed an engine can reach with the converter locked at zero output speed and the throttle fully open. It is a measure of the converter and engine condition, and is usually 2,000 to 2,800 rpm. A stall test is done only by a trained technician, for about five seconds at a time, with the brakes firmly on and the wheels chocked. A speed well below the figure suggests a weak engine or a slipping stator clutch. A speed well above it suggests slipping clutches or low pressure in the gearbox. The test heats the fluid very quickly, so it should not be repeated without cooling, and some makers forbid it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fluid temperature"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fluid temperature","Effect"],"rows":[["Below about 20 °C","Thick fluid, slow response, delayed shifts"],["70 to 100 °C","Normal working range"],["About 120 °C","Fluid starts to oxidise and wear faster"],["Above 150 °C","Fluid burns, clutches and seals harden and fail"]],"caption":"A rule of thumb is that the life of the fluid halves for each 10 °C above about 80 °C. Towing and heavy loads raise the temperature, and a cooler helps."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Do not do a stall test or check hot fluid without following the manual. Chock the wheels and keep clear of the moving parts and the exhaust."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Never overfill. Excess fluid can foam and cause slipping. Record the level and the condition on the job card."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l3", "title": "Automatic transmission hydraulics and electronic control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An automatic gearbox is a hydraulic machine, run by a computer. Pressurised fluid applies each clutch and band, and electrical solenoids decide which. Understanding how the pressure is made, controlled and routed shows why low fluid, a worn pump or a dirty valve body causes shift problems. This lesson explains the system and how it is tested."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Making pressure"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **oil pump**, usually driven by the converter housing, turns whenever the engine runs. It draws fluid from the sump through a strainer and delivers it to the valve body. A **pressure regulator valve** holds the **line pressure** at a set level, raising it with throttle opening and engine torque, to give enough force to hold the clutches without slipping, but no more than needed. Typical line pressure in drive at idle is about 3 to 6 bar, rising to 10 to 15 bar under load. Wear in the pump gears or bushes, a clogged strainer, aerated fluid or a leak at a seal lowers the pressure, and the clutches slip."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The valve body"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **valve body** is a block of channels and sliding valves. When a solenoid moves a valve, it directs pressure to a clutch. The valves are close-fitting and are moved by small pressure differences, so tiny particles of dirt or varnish make them stick. Sticking gives a delayed, harsh or missed shift. The valve body is usually bolted to the bottom of the gearbox, under the pan."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job"],"rows":[["Shift solenoids (on-off)","Open or close a pressure passage to select a gear"],["Pressure control solenoids (PWM or variable)","Vary the pressure to a clutch for smooth engagement"],["Lock-up solenoid","Controls the converter's lock-up clutch"],["Accumulator","A spring-loaded piston that cushions clutch application"],["Manual valve","Connected to the selector lever. It routes fluid for park, reverse, neutral and drive."],["Check balls and separator plate","Direct and seal the passages between layers"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electronic control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **transmission control module** (TCM), sometimes combined with the engine computer, receives signals from the **input and output speed sensors**, the **throttle or pedal position**, engine torque, the **fluid temperature sensor**, the selector position and the brake switch. It selects the gear and the clutch pressures from a shift schedule. It adapts the pressure over time as parts wear, so that shifts stay smooth. The TCM also protects the box, and goes into **limp mode**, which fixes a gear such as 3rd, if it sees a serious fault, with a warning light."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How the TCM detects clutch slip","steps":["A gearbox in 3rd gear has a ratio of 1.45. The input shaft speed sensor reads 2,900 rpm and the output shaft speed sensor reads 2,000 rpm.","Expected input speed = output speed × ratio = 2,000 × 1.45 = 2,900 rpm. The measured value matches, so there is no slip.","If the input read 3,300 rpm, the ratio would be 3,300 ÷ 2,000 = 1.65, showing a slip of about 14% on the clutch. The TCM raises the line pressure, stores a code for gear ratio error and may set limp mode."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Shift points and lock-up"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The TCM decides when to change gear using a map of vehicle speed against throttle opening. A light throttle gives early upshifts to save fuel. A heavy throttle gives later shifts for power. A kick-down, pressing the pedal to the floor, makes it change to a lower gear. Most converters lock up in the higher gears above a certain speed (about 50 to 70 km/h), to remove slip and save fuel. A lock-up clutch that does not release as you slow causes shudder or stalling, and one that does not engage causes high revs and fuel use."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing hydraulic pressure"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the fluid level and condition first. Low fluid gives low pressure.","Fit a pressure gauge, with the right hoses and adaptors, to the test port in the case, as the manual shows.","With the engine at working temperature, read the pressure in each selector position at idle, and under load, with the brakes held. Read the line pressure in reverse, which is higher.","Compare with the specification. Low pressure in all ranges suggests a worn pump, a blocked strainer or an internal leak. Low in only one range suggests a leaking clutch circuit in that range.","Remove the gauge and fit the plug with a new seal, and top up the fluid."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common faults"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely causes"],"rows":[["Delayed engagement into drive or reverse","Low fluid, low line pressure, worn clutch seals, a sticking valve"],["Slip in a gear, engine revs without acceleration","Worn clutch, low pressure, leaking seal or burnt fluid"],["Harsh shifts","High line pressure, a faulty solenoid, a bad adaptation, a stuck accumulator"],["No upshifts, stuck in one gear","Limp mode, a speed sensor, solenoid or wiring fault"],["Shudder at 50 to 70 km/h","A converter lock-up clutch fault or contaminated fluid"],["Whining noise that changes with engine speed","Pump wear, low fluid or a blocked strainer"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The gearbox can only apply its clutches as hard as its fluid pressure allows. Fluid, filter, pump, valves and sensors are the checks before any gear or clutch is blamed."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"After any repair that disturbs the valve body or the TCM, ask if the maker requires an adaptation reset, or a relearn of the shift pressures."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l4", "title": "Planetary gears and shifting", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Planetary gear sets give an automatic gearbox its gears without sliding gears in and out of mesh. This lesson explains the idea and the shift control."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The planetary gear set"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A planetary gear set has three parts. The **sun gear** is in the centre. The **planet gears** mesh with the sun and are carried on a **planet carrier**. The **ring gear (annulus)** surrounds them with internal teeth. Any of the three can be driven, held or left free."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Held","Driven","Output","Result"],"rows":[["Ring gear","Sun gear","Planet carrier","Large reduction (low gear)"],["Sun gear","Ring gear","Planet carrier","Smaller reduction"],["None (two locked together)","All together","All together","Direct drive 1:1"],["Planet carrier","Sun gear","Ring gear","Reverse (output turns the opposite way)"]],"caption":"Basic planetary gear set combinations"},
      {"t":"p","text":"By holding or driving different parts, one gear set can give several ratios. Gearboxes use two or more sets in combination to give the six, eight or more speeds."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"planetary-gearset"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Clutches and bands"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Multi-plate clutches** have discs with friction material and steel plates, clamped by a hydraulic piston, to drive a part. **Bands** are steel straps lined with friction material that wrap round a drum to hold it. **One-way clutches** hold a part from turning in one direction only. Oil pressure applies the clutches and bands, and their friction wears over time."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hydraulic and electronic control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The oil pump produces the pressure. The valve body contains valves that route the pressure to the right clutch or band for a gear. Electric **solenoids** controlled by the TCM move the valves. The TCM uses the vehicle speed, throttle position, engine load and other inputs to decide when to shift and how firmly. It can also change the **line pressure** to give smooth shifts. Many gearboxes adapt over time and store learned values."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Shift quality"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A harsh shift may mean high line pressure, a faulty solenoid or a worn valve.","A slip, or flare in revs between gears, usually means worn clutches, low pressure or low fluid.","A delay before the gear engages may mean low fluid, worn seals or worn clutches."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A typical shift schedule"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Throttle","1 to 2","2 to 3","3 to 4","4 to 5"],"rows":[["Light (about 20%)","About 15 km/h","About 30 km/h","About 45 km/h","About 60 km/h"],["Medium (about 50%)","About 30 km/h","About 50 km/h","About 75 km/h","About 100 km/h"],["Full (kick-down)","About 55 km/h","About 90 km/h","About 130 km/h","About 160 km/h"]],"caption":"Values are only an illustration of how shift points rise with throttle. The real schedule is stored in the control module. The converter's lock-up clutch usually engages in the top gears at about 55 to 80 km/h on light to medium throttle, and releases on kick-down."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Describing shift faults"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Term","What the driver feels","Typical cause"],"rows":[["Flare","Engine speed rises between gears before the next gear takes hold","Clutch slip, low pressure, worn clutch, low fluid"],["Tie-up or bind","Gearbox seems to fight itself, the engine bogs down","Two elements applied at once, solenoid or valve stuck"],["Bump or harsh shift","A jolt when the gear engages","High line pressure, a stuck accumulator, a bad adaptation"],["Hunting","The box keeps changing between two gears","A sensor fault, low throttle signal, or a calibration problem"],["Slip","The engine speed rises without matching speed","Worn clutches, low pressure, damaged seals"],["Delay","A pause before drive engages","Low fluid, low pressure, worn seals"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use these words when you write up a job. They describe exactly what happened, so that another technician can follow your finding. Note the gear, the throttle position, the road speed and whether the fluid is hot or cold."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Gear changes depend on oil pressure. Low or dirty fluid, a failing pump or worn seals all show up as shift problems."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Compare the way the gearbox shifts when cold and when warm. A fault that appears only when hot points to wear and loss of pressure."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l5", "title": "CVT and hybrid transmissions", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Not every automatic has stepped gears. A continuously variable transmission (CVT) changes its ratio smoothly, with no steps, and the transmission in a hybrid may have no gears at all. These units look very different from a conventional automatic, and need different service. This lesson explains how a CVT works, what goes wrong and how hybrid transmissions differ."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a CVT works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A belt-type CVT has two **pulleys**, each made of two cones that move together or apart. A steel belt or chain runs between them. When the cones of the **primary** (input) pulley are close together, the belt rides high on it, at a large radius. At the same time the cones of the **secondary** (output) pulley are apart and the belt rides low, at a small radius. The ratio is the radius at the output over the radius at the input. Moving the cones changes the ratio smoothly, with no gear changes. Hydraulic pressure on the pulleys sets the ratio and clamps the belt."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Ratio range of a CVT","steps":["Low ratio (pulling away): the belt runs at a radius of 25 mm on the primary pulley and 70 mm on the secondary pulley. Ratio = 70 ÷ 25 = 2.8.","High ratio (cruising): the primary radius is 65 mm and the secondary radius is 30 mm. Ratio = 30 ÷ 65 = 0.46.","The **spread** (the range from lowest to highest) is 2.8 ÷ 0.46 = 6.1. This is similar to a six-speed gearbox.","A computer picks the ratio that lets the engine run at its most efficient speed for the power required, so that the engine speed rises with the load but not always with the road speed."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Features and faults"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Feature","Notes"],"rows":[["No gear changes","Smooth acceleration. The engine speed can rise to a steady value, which some drivers find unusual."],["Torque converter or start clutch","Handles pulling away and creep"],["Special fluid (CVT fluid)","Gives the right friction between the belt and the pulleys. Only the specified fluid may be used. Never use ordinary ATF."],["Belt or chain wear","Whine, shudder, slip. The belt has to be kept clamped hard enough, so low pressure is damaging."],["Heat","A CVT is sensitive to overheating. A cooler and the correct fluid level matter a lot."],["Software","Many CVT problems are cured by updates and relearns"]]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely causes"],"rows":[["Judder pulling away","Start clutch wear, contaminated or old fluid, a software issue"],["Whining noise that rises with speed","Pump, bearings, or a worn belt and pulleys"],["Engine revs rise without road speed rising","Belt slip, low pressure, low fluid"],["Limp mode","Sensor, solenoid, overheating or low pressure"],["Delay when selecting drive","Low fluid or a worn pump or start clutch"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fluid and service"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["CVT fluid is changed at about 40,000 to 100,000 km, or sooner for towing or hot climates, as the maker says. Do not extend the intervals.","Use only the exact fluid listed. The wrong fluid damages the belt and the pulleys quickly.","Set the fluid level at the right temperature, using the scan tool's temperature reading, and follow the manual's fill method.","After a service or repair, perform the adaptation or reset procedure."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hybrid transmissions"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a power-split hybrid (module 13), the transmission often has **no stepped gears**. A planetary gear set joins the engine to an electric generator and an electric motor, and the computer changes the ratio by changing the speed of the generator. It is called an **electronic CVT** (eCVT). It has no belt, no clutches that slip and no torque converter, so it is very robust. The fluid is changed at long intervals, and some use a special fluid with good insulating properties, which must be exactly as specified."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Other hybrids use a conventional automatic, a dual-clutch gearbox, or an automated manual, with an electric motor placed between the engine and the gearbox. The motor can replace the torque converter or help the clutch. These are service jobs for a trained technician, and any work that involves high-voltage parts is covered by the safety rules of module 13."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A CVT depends on the right fluid and on temperature control. Using the wrong fluid is the commonest way to destroy one."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never mix CVT fluid with ordinary ATF, and never work on hybrid transmission components that connect to orange cables unless trained and authorised."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l6", "title": "Planetary gear ratios and how gearboxes get their gears", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A planetary gear set can give a reduction, a direct drive or a reverse, depending on which of its three members is held and which is driven. With the numbers of teeth on the gears, you can calculate the ratio, and check that a gearbox's data makes sense. This lesson shows how, and shows how combinations of a few gear sets and clutches make six, eight or more gears."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The formulas"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A simple set has a **sun gear** with S teeth, a **ring gear** with R teeth and a **carrier** that holds the planet gears. The number of teeth on the ring is the sum of those on the sun and two planets, but the ratio does not depend on the planets."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Held","Input","Output","Ratio","Effect"],"rows":[["Ring","Sun","Carrier","1 + R ÷ S","Large reduction"],["Sun","Ring","Carrier","1 + S ÷ R","Small reduction"],["Carrier","Sun","Ring","− R ÷ S","Reduction and reverse"],["Carrier","Ring","Sun","− S ÷ R","Overdrive and reverse"],["Sun","Carrier","Ring","R ÷ (R + S)","Overdrive"],["Ring","Carrier","Sun","S ÷ (R + S)","Large overdrive"],["None, any two locked together","Any","Any","1 : 1","Direct drive"]],"caption":"A negative sign means the output turns in the opposite direction."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Ratios of a gear set with S = 30 and R = 70","steps":["Ring held, sun input, carrier output: ratio = 1 + 70 ÷ 30 = 3.33. A strong reduction, used for 1st gear.","Sun held, ring input, carrier output: ratio = 1 + 30 ÷ 70 = 1.43. A smaller reduction, used for 2nd gear.","Carrier held, sun input, ring output: ratio = −70 ÷ 30 = −2.33. The minus sign shows reverse rotation.","Two parts locked together: 1.00. Direct drive, used for top gear in a simple gearbox."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a few sets give many gears"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A modern gearbox with eight forward speeds does not have eight gear sets. It has **three or four planetary sets** and **five or six shift elements** (clutches and brakes). Each gear applies a different **combination** of two or three shift elements, so the output of one set drives another. Changing gear means releasing one element and applying another."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Gear","Elements applied (example of a 6-speed)"],"rows":[["1st","Clutch A and brake 1"],["2nd","Clutch A and brake 2"],["3rd","Clutch A and clutch B"],["4th","Clutch A and clutch C"],["5th","Clutch C and clutch B"],["6th","Clutch C and brake 2"],["Reverse","Clutch D and brake 1"]],"caption":"This is an illustration. Real gearboxes have their own table in the manual. The idea is that each shift changes one element at a time."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A table of clutch application is called a **shift logic table**. You can use it to see which clutch is the cause of a fault. A gearbox that slips in 3rd, and also in 4th and 5th, has a problem with an element that those gears share. A gearbox that slips in only 1st and reverse shares brake 1, in the example above, so you look at that element. This is one of the quickest ways to narrow down an internal fault."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Finding a faulty element","steps":["A gearbox slips in 2nd and 6th but not in 1st, 3rd, 4th, 5th or reverse.","In the table, only 2nd and 6th use brake 2. Brake 2 is the common element, and the likely fault.","The gearbox needs to be opened to inspect and replace the brake plates and seals, or a reconditioned unit is fitted."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"One-way clutches and overrun"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **one-way clutch** (freewheel) locks a member in one direction and frees it in the other. It gives a smooth change without a clutch being released at exactly the right moment, and it gives no engine braking in that gear, as it frees on overrun. In low gear selected by the lever (L or 1), another clutch may be applied to give engine braking down a hill. A failing one-way clutch gives a bang or slip when changing, or no drive."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The gear ratios are fixed by the tooth counts. What changes is which members are held or driven. The shift logic table tells you which element is used in each gear."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Print the clutch application table for the gearbox you are working on. It turns a slipping gear into a short list of suspect parts."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l7", "title": "Differentials and final drive", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The differential is the last stage before the wheels. It reduces the speed, turns the drive through 90° on rear- and four-wheel drives, and lets the wheels turn at different speeds. This lesson explains how."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"differential"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Crown wheel and pinion"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **pinion** gear on the propeller shaft meshes with the larger **crown wheel (ring gear)**, which turns the drive through a right angle and gives the final drive reduction. The teeth are usually **hypoid** shaped to be quiet and strong. The pinion and crown wheel are matched sets and must be adjusted correctly for **backlash** (the small clearance between the teeth) and tooth contact pattern."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The open differential"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When a vehicle turns a corner, the outside wheel travels farther than the inside wheel, so it must turn faster. In an **open differential**, the crown wheel carries a cage with **spider (planet) gears** and two **side gears** attached to the axle shafts. The spider gears can rotate, so one shaft can turn faster than the other, while the cage drives them both. The torque going to each wheel is equal, however, so if one wheel has no grip, the other gets little drive."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Limited-slip and locking differentials"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Limited-slip differential (LSD):** uses clutch plates, special gears or viscous fluid to send some torque to the wheel with grip.","**Locking differential:** can be locked by the driver or automatically, so that both wheels turn at the same speed. It is good for rough ground but should be unlocked on hard roads."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil and noise"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Differentials use gear oil of a type and viscosity in the manual, often an API GL-5 grade, and **limited-slip units need a special friction modifier**. Check the level at the fill plug. A **whine** that varies with load can mean worn gears or poor backlash adjustment. A **clunk** can mean excess backlash or worn spider gears. A **howl** or rumble can mean worn bearings."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Noise","Possible cause"],"rows":[["Whine on acceleration or coast","Wrong gear mesh or worn gears, low oil"],["Clunk taking up drive","Excess backlash, worn U-joints or axle splines"],["Rumble that changes with speed","Worn pinion or carrier bearings"],["Chatter or grabbing in turns","Limited-slip unit with wrong oil or worn clutch plates"]],"caption":"Differential noises"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking a differential"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check the oil level and condition. Metal particles on the magnet show wear. Water makes the oil milky.","Check the pinion and axle seals for leaks, and the breather for blockage.","Listen on a road test. A whine in drive that changes in coast suggests the crown wheel and pinion. A clunk when you engage drive suggests backlash in the gears or joints. A growl that changes when turning suggests the side bearings.","With the vehicle raised and safely supported, turn the pinion flange by hand and feel for play. A small amount of backlash is normal."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Differentials are set up with fine tolerances. Setting the pinion depth and backlash correctly is a specialist job, but checking the oil and listening are tasks for every technician."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When you change differential oil, look at the drain plug magnet. Fine grey paste is normal. Chips and large pieces are not."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l8", "title": "Differentials and limited-slip systems in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A differential seems simple, but it explains many handling problems, noises and the odd behaviour of a vehicle with one wheel on a slippery surface. This lesson goes deeper into how it shares speed and torque, how limited-slip types work, and how to test and service them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed and torque in an open differential"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An open differential has a simple rule. The **average speed** of the two wheels equals the speed of the carrier (the housing driven by the crown wheel). If one wheel goes faster, the other goes slower by the same amount. The torque is always shared **equally**, because the gears act like a balanced lever. This is the key point: the torque on each wheel is limited by the grip of the wheel with the **least** traction."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Carrier speed = (left wheel speed + right wheel speed) ÷ 2"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Cornering with an open differential","steps":["The carrier turns at 1,000 rpm. On a left turn, the left (inner) wheel travels slower, at 950 rpm.","Right wheel speed = 2 × 1,000 − 950 = 1,050 rpm. The outer wheel goes faster by the same amount that the inner one goes slower.","If one wheel is on ice and spins, the carrier speed stays at 1,000, but the spinning wheel could be at 2,000 rpm and the other at 0 rpm, so the vehicle does not move. The torque at the spinning wheel is very low, so the torque at the other wheel is limited to the same low value."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Limited-slip and locking types"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Features"],"rows":[["Clutch-pack (mechanical LSD)","Springs and the side thrust of the gears squeeze friction plates between the carrier and the side gears, so a speed difference is resisted","Strong lock. Wears out with use. Needs a special oil with a friction modifier."],["Torsen (worm gear)","Worm gears that cannot be driven back from the wheels, so the one with more traction takes more torque","Smooth, no friction plates, no wear. Torque bias of about 2:1 to 4:1."],["Viscous coupling","A sealed unit of plates in a thick silicone fluid. A speed difference heats the fluid, which thickens and locks.","Smooth, gradual lock. Wears and weakens with age."],["Electronically controlled clutch (e-LSD)","A computer presses a clutch pack to lock the sides, based on wheel speed sensors","Adjustable. Needs the correct fluid and software."],["Brake-based (electronic) differential","The brake of the spinning wheel is applied by the ABS unit, so torque shifts to the wheel with grip","No extra parts. Brakes work harder, and may overheat on long spins."],["Locking differential","A driver-selected or automatic lock that connects both axle shafts","Gives full traction. Not for use on dry hard roads or at speed."]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"What a torque bias ratio means","steps":["A Torsen differential has a torque bias ratio of 3:1. The wheel with less grip can take 100 N·m before slipping.","The wheel with more grip can then take up to 3 × 100 = 300 N·m, so the total is 400 N·m. An open differential would give only 100 + 100 = 200 N·m.","The limited-slip type therefore pulls away on mixed surfaces where an open one would spin one wheel."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing a limited-slip differential"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Turning torque test:** with the vehicle raised, one wheel off the ground, in neutral, use a torque wrench on the wheel nut to turn that wheel while the other is held. Clutch-type units need a minimum torque, as the manual says. An open diff turns with very little force.","**Symptoms of a worn clutch-type LSD:** chatter or judder when turning tight corners at low speed, a groan or grabbing noise, and loss of traction.","**Oil:** use the exact oil, with the friction modifier. A wrong oil causes chatter. Never add additive unless the maker says so."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Setting up and noise"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Noise","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Whine in drive, changes on the overrun","Pinion or crown wheel contact pattern or backlash"],["Whine on the overrun only","Pinion preload or backlash on the coast side"],["Rumble that changes when turning","Side bearings or carrier bearing"],["Clunk on taking up the drive","Backlash, worn side gears or a loose pinion flange"],["Chatter on tight turns","Worn LSD clutches or wrong oil"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"An open differential sends torque equally to both wheels, so the wheel with less grip decides how much force you get. Limited-slip units overcome this in different ways."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When a customer says a car 'spins one wheel', first check whether it has an open differential, and the condition of the tyres on the driven axle. A good set of tyres matters more than any other change."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l9", "title": "Four-wheel and all-wheel drive", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Four-wheel-drive (4WD) and all-wheel-drive (AWD) systems send power to all four wheels. They give better traction, but need care in use and service."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Transfer case:** a gearbox behind the main gearbox that splits the drive between the front and rear axles. Many have a **low range**, a set of reduction gears for slow, heavy work such as steep hills and towing.","**Centre differential or coupling:** lets the front and rear axles turn at slightly different speeds. Types include a viscous coupling, an electronically controlled clutch or a locking differential.","**Front and rear differentials and driveshafts:** the same as in a two-wheel-drive vehicle, with an extra shaft to the front axle.","**Locking hubs:** on some vehicles, hubs on the front wheels are locked in 4WD and unlocked for normal driving, which reduces wear."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Part-time and full-time systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **part-time 4WD** has no centre differential. The front and rear shafts are locked together, so the axles must turn at the same average speed. On hard, dry surfaces, the front and rear wheels travel different distances in a turn, and the driveline **winds up**, putting heavy stress on the parts, with hopping and tyre scrub. Use part-time 4WD only on loose, wet or slippery surfaces. A **full-time 4WD or AWD** has a centre differential or coupling, and can be used on any surface."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tyre matching"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On a vehicle with AWD or full-time 4WD, all four tyres must be the same size, type and have similar wear. A small difference in circumference makes the centre coupling work constantly, causing it to overheat and wear. Do not fit a tyre of a different size, or one new tyre with three worn ones, unless the manual allows."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Towing and service"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many 4WD and AWD vehicles must not be towed with one axle on the road, because the driveline can be damaged. Use a flatbed or follow the manual. Transfer case and differential oils must be changed at the intervals, and the correct oil type used."},
      {"t":"table","head":["System","Centre differential?","Use on hard dry roads?"],"rows":[["Part-time 4WD","No","No, risk of wind-up"],["Full-time 4WD","Yes or coupling","Yes"],["AWD (on demand)","Coupling that sends power when needed","Yes"]],"caption":"Four-wheel drive types"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Modes of a typical 4WD"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Mode","What it means","When to use it"],"rows":[["2H","Two-wheel drive, high range","Normal dry roads"],["4H","Four-wheel drive, high range. Centre locked.","Loose surfaces, mud, snow. Not on dry hard roads."],["4L","Four-wheel drive, low range. A reduction gear multiplies torque.","Very steep or soft ground, towing a heavy load slowly"],["Auto 4WD","System sends torque to the axle that needs it","Mixed conditions"],["Neutral","Both ranges disconnected","Towing the vehicle with the wheels turning (some makers)"]],"caption":"Follow the owner's manual: modes differ between models, and you must not use a part-time 4WD on hard dry roads."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A mismatch in tyre size on a four-wheel drive can cause damage that looks like a drivetrain fault. Check the tyres first."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Use low range for slow, heavy work, not for speed. Select it only as the manual says, usually when stationary or moving very slowly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l10", "title": "All-wheel drive systems in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"All-wheel drive vehicles are more varied than they first appear. Some use a heavy transfer case with low range for off-road. Others use a small electronically controlled clutch to send some power to the rear wheels when the front wheels slip. Each type has its own faults, and the most common fault for all of them is a mismatch in tyre size. This lesson explains the main systems and the checks to make."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of system"},
      {"t":"table","head":["System","How power reaches the second axle","Typical use"],"rows":[["Part-time 4WD with transfer case","A driver-selected transfer case locks the front and rear shafts together. It has high and low range.","Off-road pick-ups and SUVs"],["Full-time 4WD","A centre differential allows the front and rear shafts to turn at different speeds. It may be locked.","Mixed-road and off-road vehicles"],["Clutch-based AWD (on demand)","A multi-plate clutch or coupling in the line to one axle, controlled by a computer or by speed difference","Most family cars and crossovers"],["Electric AWD","A separate electric motor drives one axle, with no mechanical link","Hybrids and electric vehicles"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Centre differentials and couplings"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Open centre differential:** splits torque equally. Needs locking or braking for traction.","**Torsen or planetary with bias:** splits torque unequally, more to the rear, and bias to the axle with more grip.","**Viscous coupling:** sends torque to the second axle when there is a speed difference.","**Multi-plate electronic coupling:** a hydraulic pump and a clutch pack, controlled by the computer, which uses wheel speed, throttle, steering angle and sometimes yaw sensors. It can send most of the torque rearward within a fraction of a second. The oil in the unit must be changed at the interval, because worn oil causes slip and heat.","**Transfer case:** a box on the gearbox output. In 4WD it drives the two propeller shafts, and in low range a planetary gear set reduces the speed to give more torque for crawling."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The problem of wind-up"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a part-time 4WD with the centre locked, the front and rear axles are forced to turn at the same speed. On a hard road, in a turn, the front wheels travel further than the rears, so the shafts are twisted against each other, and the driveline **winds up**. The tyres skip and squeal, the steering feels tight, and the driveline is stressed. This is why a part-time 4WD must not be used in 4H on dry roads. On loose surfaces, the tyres slip and release the wind-up."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tyre matching"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How a small tyre difference loads an AWD system","steps":["Front tyres have a rolling circumference of 1.985 m. A rear tyre of a different make with a circumference of 2.050 m is fitted. The difference is 2.050 ÷ 1.985 = 1.033, or 3.3%.","At 100 km/h on the rear, the front axle needs to turn 3.3% faster, so the front wheels travel at 96.8 km/h if they turn at the same rate as the rear. The couplings must therefore slip continuously by about 3 km/h.","A clutch-based coupling takes the slip as constant heat and wear. A viscous coupling heats up and may lock, and the centre differential clutch wears and causes judder or a binding sensation in turns.","A tread depth difference of 4 mm on a tyre changes the circumference by about 25 mm, or 1.2%, which is also significant on some systems. Many makers state a maximum difference of just a few millimetres in tread depth between tyres."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Service and checks"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Always keep all four tyres the **same make, model, size and similar wear**, and replace them as a set where the maker advises.","Change the oil in the transfer case, the rear differential and any electronic coupling at the intervals, with the exact fluid.","Check the **propeller shafts, joints and centre bearings** for play and vibration.","Check for **leaks** at the transfer case and axle seals.","Use a **scan tool** to read the state of the system, the wheel speeds, the clutch command and any codes.","Do not tow with some wheels on the ground, or run on a rolling road (dynamometer) with only one axle turning, unless the maker allows it. The coupling can overheat or the vehicle can drive off the rollers."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely causes"],"rows":[["Binding, squealing tyres on tight turns on a hard road","Part-time 4WD in 4H, tyre mismatch, or a worn centre coupling"],["Vibration under acceleration","Worn propeller shaft joints, a bent shaft or a failing centre bearing"],["Whine or growl that changes in 4L or 4H","Transfer case chain or gear wear, low oil"],["Warning light for AWD or traction","Wheel speed sensor, coupling fault, low oil, or a tyre size problem"],["Rear wheels do not drive on slip","Failed coupling, low fluid, blown fuse or pump fault in the on-demand unit"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Tyres are part of an AWD driveline. A mismatch in size causes wear, heat and damage that is easy to prevent."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When a customer buys a single replacement tyre for an AWD car, check the tread depths and advise on replacing in pairs or sets, or shaving a new tyre to match, as the maker allows."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l11", "title": "Transmission faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Automatic transmission faults are common, and the repair is often costly. This lesson shows you how to recognise the signs, what to check and what a workshop can and can not do."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common symptoms"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Possible causes"],"rows":[["Slipping (engine revs without the vehicle accelerating)","Low or burnt fluid, worn clutches, low line pressure"],["Delayed engagement (a pause before D or R engages)","Low fluid, worn seals, worn clutches, blocked filter"],["Harsh shifts","Incorrect fluid, faulty solenoid, high line pressure, adaptation errors"],["No upshift or stuck in one gear","Failed sensor or solenoid, limp mode, wiring fault"],["Shudder at light throttle at 50 to 80 km/h","Converter lock-up clutch problem, wrong or old fluid"],["Overheating","Blocked cooler or lines, low fluid, towing, worn clutches"]],"caption":"Automatic transmission symptoms"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fluid smell and colour"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Check the fluid first, because it often gives the answer. A burnt smell and dark colour show overheating and clutch wear. Pink milky fluid shows coolant in the gearbox, usually through a leaking cooler in the radiator. This is serious, because coolant ruins the clutches."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Warning lights and codes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A transmission warning lamp, flashing gear indicator or limp mode shows a fault. Use a scan tool to read the codes, which often begin with P07xx for the transmission. Read the live data, such as input and output speed, the line pressure, the temperature and the converter slip. A code points to a circuit, not necessarily to a worn part. Check the wiring, connectors and fluid before blaming the gearbox."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a workshop can and can not do"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Normally within scope:** fluid and filter service, checking and repairing leaks, replacing external sensors, speed sensors, solenoids and some valve body parts, wiring repair, cooler line repair and software updates, using the correct tools and procedure.","**Specialist or exchange:** internal repairs, rebuilds, replacing clutches and torque converters, and CVT or dual-clutch internal repair. Many shops fit a rebuilt or exchange unit."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Service intervals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The manual gives intervals for fluid and filter, and for severe use such as towing, hot climates, stop-start traffic and mountain roads. Some gearboxes are described as 'filled for life', but fluid still ages, and many technicians recommend a change at a sensible distance."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A diagnostic order"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Interview the customer: when does it happen, in what gear and when hot or cold? Has the car been towing? When was the fluid last changed?","Check the **fluid level and condition** exactly as the manual states, with the engine at the right temperature.","Check for **codes** in the transmission and engine modules, and for software updates.","Check **external parts**: wiring connectors, sensors, selector cable adjustment, mounts and leaks.","**Road test** and read live data: speeds of the input and output shafts, the slip of the converter, the commanded and actual line pressure and the gear.","Perform the **hydraulic pressure test** if the manual calls for it, with the gauge at the test port.","Only then decide whether the fault is electrical, hydraulic or mechanical."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Be careful with hot transmission fluid and with the road test. If the gearbox fails to engage a gear, do not force it. Use the correct recovery method."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Start with the cheap, simple checks: fluid level and condition, codes, wiring and the cooler. They solve many complaints."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m17-l12", "title": "Automatic transmission service and checks", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A fluid and filter service is the most common automatic transmission job and the one most often done badly. The wrong fluid, a wrong level or contamination can destroy a gearbox in weeks. This lesson gives the procedures and checks for a safe service, and explains what a workshop should and should not attempt."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking the fluid level"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Procedures differ, and the manual must be followed. A few general rules apply to a **dipstick** type."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Park on level ground, with the handbrake on, and run the engine until the fluid reaches working temperature, usually after a drive of a few kilometres.","With the engine running at idle, move the selector through all positions, pausing in each, and return to park or neutral as the manual says.","Pull the dipstick, wipe it clean, push it all the way in and pull it out again. Read the level on the hot side of the markings.","Check the colour and smell. Bright red or pink and clean is good. Dark brown or black with a burnt smell shows overheating and wear. Milky fluid shows water.","Add fluid slowly through the dipstick tube, a little at a time, to avoid overfilling."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern gearboxes have **no dipstick** and are filled through a **level plug** in the pan, with the fluid at a stated temperature, with a scan tool reading the temperature. The plug is opened with the engine running and the car level, and the fluid should just trickle out. Overfilling or underfilling causes foaming, slipping and damage."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Draining and refilling"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Warm the gearbox slightly, so that the fluid drains easily, but not so hot as to burn you.","Raise the vehicle safely and level. Place a large container under the pan, because a gearbox pan holds several litres.","Remove the drain plug if fitted, or loosen the pan bolts and let the fluid out gradually.","Remove the pan. Look at the debris in the pan and on the magnet. A fine, grey paste is normal wear. Large pieces of metal or friction material show internal damage.","Replace the **filter** and its seal, and clean the pan and the magnet. Fit a new pan gasket, and tighten the bolts to the specified torque, evenly.","Refill with the **exact fluid** specified, to the correct level, using the manual's procedure.","Run the engine, shift through all positions and recheck the level."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Method","What it does","Notes"],"rows":[["Drain and fill","Replaces only the fluid in the pan, about 30 to 50% of the total","Gentle. Repeat after a short drive to renew more."],["Machine fluid exchange","A machine replaces the fluid through the cooler lines with new fluid, nearly all of it","Needs the right machine and the exact fluid. Follow the maker's advice."],["Full overhaul","The gearbox is stripped and rebuilt","For a worn or damaged unit"]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much does a drain and fill renew?","steps":["A gearbox holds 8 litres in total and a drain and fill removes 3 litres of old fluid. After refilling, the fluid is 3 ÷ 8 = 37.5% new.","After a second drain and fill with 3 litres, the old fluid left is 5 litres, which mixes with 3 litres of new, so after draining 3 of the 8, about 5 × (5 ÷ 8) = 3.1 litres of the original remains. The fluid is about 61% new.","Three drains give about 76% new fluid. This is why some makers advise repeated services in stages for an older gearbox."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking the cooler"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The transmission cooler, often inside the radiator tank, removes heat. A blocked cooler overheats the gearbox. A leak inside the radiator mixes coolant and fluid, which destroys a gearbox quickly. To test the flow, disconnect the return line and run the engine, catching the fluid in a measuring jug. The flow should be about a litre in 20 to 30 seconds, as the manual says. Check the radiator for cross-contamination if the fluid is milky and the coolant is oily."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a workshop can and cannot do"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Task","For a general workshop","For a specialist"],"rows":[["Fluid and filter change","Yes, with the right fluid and procedure","Optional"],["Cooler lines and cooler checks","Yes","Optional"],["Speed sensor and solenoid replacement","Yes, if accessible and the manual allows","Optional"],["Valve body clean or replace","Possible with training and clean conditions","Often"],["Torque converter and clutch overhaul","No","Yes"],["Programming and adaptation","If you have the tool and procedure","Yes, for complex cases"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Gearbox fluid and the filter are the most important maintenance items. Use the exact specification, the exact level and the right procedure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Hot transmission fluid causes bad burns, and a gearbox is heavy. Take care with the fluid temperature, and use a gearbox jack for heavy parts."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Check transmission fluid, read transmission data and check the differential","objective":"Inspect the transmission fluid level and condition, read transmission data with a scan tool, and check the differential oil level and play.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["Vehicle with an automatic transmission and a differential, on a lift or level ground","Scan tool that reads transmission data","Clean white rag or paper, funnel and fluid specified in the manual","Drain container, socket set and torque wrench","Workshop manual and notepad"],"safety":["Chock the wheels and apply the handbrake. Support the vehicle on a lift or stands.","Keep clear of the exhaust, the fans and belts when the engine is running.","Hot fluid can cause burns, so wear gloves and glasses.","Follow the manual for the checking procedure and do not do a stall test.","Wipe up fluid spills immediately and dispose of waste correctly."],"steps":["Find the fluid type, level procedure and specification in the manual.","Bring the engine and transmission to the temperature that the manual states.","With the vehicle level and the correct selector position, pull the dipstick or open the check plug as the manual says.","Read the level, and record the colour and smell of the fluid on the white rag.","Connect the scan tool and read any transmission codes.","View live data for the input speed, output speed and temperature.","Note whether the gear selected agrees with the vehicle speed and the engine speed.","Raise the vehicle safely and find the differential fill plug.","Open the fill plug and check that the oil is level with the bottom of the hole.","Hold a wheel or the propeller shaft and feel for play, noting any clunk.","Refit the plug to the specified torque and clean the area.","Record all results and write a short condition report."],"checks":["I checked the fluid level in the correct way for this vehicle.","I described the colour and smell of the fluid and what it shows.","I read the transmission data and codes with the scan tool.","I checked the differential oil level and play.","I worked safely and followed the manual."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m17-q01","q":"What is the job of the torque converter?","options":["To select the gear in the gearbox using friction bands and a valve body","To pump oil through the valve body at all times, whatever the speed","To connect the engine to the gearbox by fluid and to multiply torque at low speed","To lock the output shaft when the vehicle is parked on a slope"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l1","explain":"It replaces the clutch in an automatic vehicle."},
    {"id":"m17-q02","q":"What does the park pawl do?","options":["It cools the transmission fluid as it returns from the radiator","It selects first gear when the vehicle moves off from rest","It locks the output shaft so the vehicle cannot roll","It connects the engine to the converter when the lever is in drive"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l1","explain":"It engages in P and is released in all other positions."},
    {"id":"m17-q03","q":"What operates the clutches and bands in a conventional automatic transmission?","options":["A belt driven by the engine that squeezes each clutch pack","Cables from the gear lever pulling on each clutch directly","Hydraulic pressure controlled by the valve body and solenoids","A clutch pedal operated by the driver on every gear change"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l1","explain":"The TCM operates solenoids that route the pressure."},
    {"id":"m17-q04","q":"How does a CVT change its ratio?","options":["It uses two clutches that engage odd and even gears in turn","It uses a torque converter with no gears or pulleys at all","It uses a set of fixed gears that are selected by the driver or a computer","A belt or chain runs between two pulleys whose widths change"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l1","explain":"A CVT has no fixed gears and the ratio varies smoothly."},
    {"id":"m17-q05","q":"What does selector position P do?","options":["It disconnects the engine from the road wheels","It locks the output shaft with a pawl","It engages first gear","It switches off the engine"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l1","explain":"Park locks the output shaft mechanically. Neutral disconnects the drive but does not lock it."},
    {"id":"m17-q06","q":"What is the turbine in a torque converter attached to?","options":["The differential and the final drive gears","The engine crankshaft through the drive plate","The gearbox input shaft","The oil pump at the front of the converter"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"The impeller is on the engine side and the turbine is on the gearbox side."},
    {"id":"m17-q07","q":"What does the stator do?","options":["It redirects the fluid at low speed to multiply torque","It locks the converter to the engine at cruising speed","It cools the converter by sending fluid to the radiator","It pumps the fluid out to the clutches and bands"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"It sits on a one-way clutch and freewheels at higher speed."},
    {"id":"m17-q08","q":"What does the lock-up clutch do?","options":["It disconnects the engine from the gearbox when idling at a stop","It stops the vehicle rolling back when stopped on a hill","It locks the turbine to the housing at cruising speed to remove slip","It changes the gear ratio in the planetary gear sets"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"Locking removes converter slip at cruising speed, which saves fuel and reduces the heat built up in the fluid."},
    {"id":"m17-q09","q":"Why must the correct automatic transmission fluid be used?","options":["The fluid only cools the gearbox, so any oil will do the job","Fluids differ in friction properties, and the wrong one can cause slipping and damage","The wrong fluid only affects the dipstick reading and nothing else","All fluids are the same, only the colours and prices differ"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"Follow the specification in the manufacturer's workshop manual, because friction properties differ between fluids."},
    {"id":"m17-q10","q":"The ATF on the dipstick is dark brown and smells burnt. What does this indicate?","options":["A new gearbox that is still running in normally","Overheating and worn clutch material","Fluid in perfect condition that has just been changed","Coolant mixed into the fluid from a leaking cooler"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"Check for the cause of the overheating, such as a blocked cooler."},
    {"id":"m17-q11","q":"The ATF looks pink and milky. What is the likely cause?","options":["Coolant is leaking into the gearbox, for example from a radiator cooler","The gearbox is overfilled and the fluid is aerated","The fluid has cooled down after a long journey","The fluid is brand new and is simply the normal colour for fresh fluid from the can"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"Coolant ruins the clutches quickly, so find the leak at once."},
    {"id":"m17-q12","q":"What is stall speed?","options":["The speed of the vehicle at the moment the engine stalls","The maximum engine speed reached with the vehicle held and the throttle fully open","The lowest idle speed reached with the air conditioning on and the selector in park","The speed at which the engine stops running at idle"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"A stall test is done only as the manual describes, because it creates heat."},
    {"id":"m17-q13","q":"A fluid temperature of about 150 °C is reached. What is the effect?","options":["The fluid burns and parts fail","The fluid becomes thicker and better","The gearbox runs more efficiently","Nothing at all happens"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l2","explain":"Above about 120 °C the fluid degrades quickly. Above 150 °C it burns, and the damage to clutches and seals is permanent."},
    {"id":"m17-q14","q":"What does the pressure regulator valve control?","options":["The engine coolant temperature","The tyre pressure","The fuel pressure","The line pressure"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l3","explain":"The regulator sets line pressure, which is raised with load to hold the clutches without slip."},
    {"id":"m17-q15","q":"Input speed is 3,300 rpm and output is 2,000 rpm in a gear with ratio 1.45. What does this show?","options":["No slip at all in this gear","A faulty speedometer cable","Clutch slip of about 14%","A flat battery in the car"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l3","explain":"Expected input is 2,000 × 1.45 = 2,900 rpm. The extra shows slip: the actual ratio is 1.65."},
    {"id":"m17-q16","q":"What often causes a delayed or harsh shift from a sticking valve?","options":["Low tyre pressure on the rear","A blocked cabin filter in the dash","A dirty windscreen on the car","Dirt or varnish in the valves"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l3","explain":"The valves fit very closely and move on small pressure differences, so tiny particles make them stick."},
    {"id":"m17-q17","q":"What does a kick-down do?","options":["It locks the transmission in park","It switches off the engine","A change to a lower gear","It applies the brakes"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l3","explain":"Pressing the pedal to the floor signals the TCM to choose a lower gear for more power."},
    {"id":"m17-q18","q":"What is the first check when an automatic is slow to engage drive?","options":["The fluid level and condition","The brake pad thickness at the front","The tyre tread depth on all four","The wiper blades and washer jets"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l3","explain":"Low or aerated fluid gives low pressure and slow engagement. It is the cheapest and quickest check."},
    {"id":"m17-q19","q":"What are the three parts of a planetary gear set?","options":["Pinion, crown wheel and spider gears with a differential case","Impeller, turbine and stator with a lock-up clutch","Input shaft, layshaft and output shaft with selector forks","Sun gear, planet carrier with planet gears, and ring gear"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l4","explain":"Holding or driving different parts gives different ratios."},
    {"id":"m17-q20","q":"In a planetary gear set, what gives direct drive at 1:1?","options":["Holding the sun gear and driving the ring gear to turn it slower","Holding the carrier and driving the sun gear to reverse it","Holding the ring gear and driving the sun gear to turn it faster","Two parts locked together so the whole set turns as one"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l4","explain":"With two members locked, the third has to turn at the same speed."},
    {"id":"m17-q21","q":"What do clutches and bands do in an automatic gearbox?","options":["They keep the fluid cool as it circulates through the cooler","They filter the transmission fluid before it reaches the valve body","They transmit power from the engine to the torque converter","They hold or drive parts of the planetary gear sets to select a gear"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l4","explain":"They are applied by oil pressure from the valve body, and each combination holds or drives a part of the gear sets."},
    {"id":"m17-q22","q":"What does a flare in engine revs between gears usually indicate?","options":["A perfectly normal shift that needs no further checks","Slipping clutches, low pressure or low fluid","A worn spark plug causing a misfire at idle","A blocked air filter restricting the engine breathing"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l4","explain":"Check the fluid level and condition first, then pressure and codes, because slipping clutches and low pressure cause a flare."},
    {"id":"m17-q23","q":"A CVT pulley runs at 25 mm primary radius and 70 mm secondary radius. What is the ratio?","options":["0.36","1.0","45","2.8"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l5","explain":"Ratio = secondary radius ÷ primary radius = 70 ÷ 25 = 2.8, a low ratio for pulling away."},
    {"id":"m17-q24","q":"What fluid must be used in a CVT?","options":["Any automatic transmission fluid","Only the CVT fluid specified","Engine oil of the right grade","Brake fluid from a sealed can"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l5","explain":"The belt and pulleys depend on a specific friction. The wrong fluid damages them quickly."},
    {"id":"m17-q25","q":"What is an eCVT in a hybrid?","options":["A belt drive with two pulleys","A dual clutch gearbox","A manual gearbox with a computer","A planetary set with no belt"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l5","explain":"The computer changes the ratio by changing generator speed. There is no belt or stepped gear."},
    {"id":"m17-q26","q":"A gear set has S = 30 and R = 70. What is the ratio with the ring held, sun input and carrier output?","options":["1.43","3.33","2.33","0.30"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l6","explain":"Ratio = 1 + R ÷ S = 1 + 70 ÷ 30 = 3.33. A strong reduction."},
    {"id":"m17-q27","q":"Same gear set, sun held, ring input and carrier output. What is the ratio?","options":["1.43","3.33","0.70","2.33"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l6","explain":"Ratio = 1 + S ÷ R = 1 + 30 ÷ 70 = 1.43. This is a smaller reduction than with the ring held, and it is typically used for second gear."},
    {"id":"m17-q28","q":"A gearbox slips only in 2nd and 6th. What does a shift logic table help you find?","options":["The tyre size","The element common to both","The engine oil grade","The brake pedal travel"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l6","explain":"The shift logic table shows which clutches and brakes are used in each gear. The element common to the slipping gears is the suspect."},
    {"id":"m17-q29","q":"What does a negative ratio mean in a planetary set?","options":["The output turns faster than input","The input is fully disconnected","Reverse rotation of the output","The oil has run out of the box"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l6","explain":"A negative sign shows reverse rotation. Holding the carrier and driving the sun gives reverse."},
    {"id":"m17-q30","q":"What does the pinion gear do in a differential?","options":["It locks the two wheels together so they turn as one","It lets the wheels turn at different speeds in a corner","It meshes with the crown wheel and turns the drive through a right angle","It cools the gear oil by splashing it across the case"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l7","explain":"Together with the crown wheel it gives the final drive reduction."},
    {"id":"m17-q31","q":"Why is a differential needed?","options":["The outside wheel travels farther in a turn, so the wheels must turn at different speeds","It gives the vehicle a higher top speed on the motorway","It stops the wheels from spinning on a slippery surface","It supplies the oil to the axle bearings and brakes"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l7","explain":"An open differential lets the wheels speed differ while sharing the drive."},
    {"id":"m17-q32","q":"What is the weakness of an open differential?","options":["It needs no oil because it has very few moving parts inside the case","If one wheel has no grip, the other gets little drive","It makes the vehicle slower in a straight line","It cannot turn corners without damage to the tyres"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l7","explain":"Equal torque goes to each wheel, so the wheel with least grip limits the drive."},
    {"id":"m17-q33","q":"What does a limited-slip differential do?","options":["It locks both wheels solid at all times, like a rigid axle","It cools the driveshaft by pumping oil along its length","It lets the wheels turn with no drive when one slips","It sends some torque to the wheel with grip when the other slips"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l7","explain":"It uses clutch plates, gears or viscous fluid."},
    {"id":"m17-q34","q":"What is backlash?","options":["The torque applied to the pinion nut when tightening","The oil level in the differential after a long drive","The small clearance between meshing gear teeth","The noise made by the gears when the engine is at idle"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l7","explain":"It must be within the specification for quiet and long-lasting gears."},
    {"id":"m17-q35","q":"What special requirement do limited-slip differentials often have?","options":["Heated oil supplied from a separate cooler circuit","A different type of fill plug with a fine gauze filter","No oil at all, because the clutches run dry","A friction modifier in the oil"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l7","explain":"Without it, the clutch plates can chatter."},
    {"id":"m17-q36","q":"The carrier of an open differential turns at 1,000 rpm. The left wheel turns at 950 rpm. What does the right wheel do?","options":["950 rpm","1,000 rpm","1,050 rpm","2,000 rpm"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l8","explain":"The average of the two wheels equals the carrier speed. Right = 2 × 1,000 − 950 = 1,050 rpm."},
    {"id":"m17-q37","q":"Why does an open differential give poor traction with one wheel on ice?","options":["Torque is limited by the low-grip wheel","Torque goes only to the wheel with more grip","The differential locks completely","The engine stops delivering power"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l8","explain":"The torque is shared equally, so the wheel on ice, which can take little torque, limits the torque to the other."},
    {"id":"m17-q38","q":"A Torsen differential has a bias ratio of 3:1. The low-grip wheel takes 100 N·m. What is the most the other wheel can take?","options":["100 N·m","33 N·m","3 N·m","300 N·m"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l8","explain":"The high-grip wheel can take up to the bias ratio times the low-grip wheel's torque, 3 × 100 = 300 N·m."},
    {"id":"m17-q39","q":"A clutch-type LSD chatters on tight turns. What is a likely cause?","options":["Worn clutches or the wrong oil","A flat spare tyre in the boot","A weak battery under the bonnet","A blocked air filter in the box"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l8","explain":"The clutch plates need the correct oil to slip smoothly. The wrong oil or worn plates make them grab and release."},
    {"id":"m17-q40","q":"What is the transfer case in a four-wheel-drive vehicle?","options":["A gearbox that splits drive between the front and rear axles and often has low range","A box that stores the spare wheel and jack under the floor","A cooler for the transmission fluid fitted in front of the radiator","A case that holds the fuel pump and the fuel gauge sender"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l9","explain":"It is mounted behind the main gearbox and splits drive between the axles, often with a low range for heavy work."},
    {"id":"m17-q41","q":"Why should part-time 4WD not be used on dry tarmac?","options":["The transfer case will fill with air and lose its oil","The driveline winds up because the axles are locked and turn at the same average speed","The tyres will become flat from the extra heat they make","The fuel tank will overflow because of the extra drive load"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l9","explain":"Wind-up causes hopping, tyre scrub and damage."},
    {"id":"m17-q42","q":"What does a centre differential or coupling do in a full-time system?","options":["It lets the front and rear axles turn at slightly different speeds","It drives the alternator and the power steering pump","It cools the transfer case by circulating the gear oil","It locks the front and rear axles together at all times"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l9","explain":"It prevents driveline wind-up on hard surfaces by letting the front and rear axles turn at slightly different speeds."},
    {"id":"m17-q43","q":"Why must all four tyres match on an AWD vehicle?","options":["A difference in size makes the centre coupling work constantly and overheat","It increases the fuel economy a lot on long journeys on the motorway at speed","It reduces the noise from the engine at higher speeds","It improves the appearance of the vehicle on the road"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l9","explain":"Tyres must match in size, type and similar wear, because a rolling-radius difference makes the centre coupling work constantly."},
    {"id":"m17-q44","q":"What is low range used for?","options":["Saving fuel at cruising speed on a long journey","Slow, heavy work such as steep hills and towing","Charging the battery when the vehicle is stationary","Fast driving on motorways in good weather conditions"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l9","explain":"It multiplies the torque for low-speed work."},
    {"id":"m17-q45","q":"What mode of a 4WD vehicle uses low-range gearing?","options":["4L","2H","4H","Auto"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l9","explain":"4L is four-wheel drive, low range, for very steep or soft ground at low speeds."},
    {"id":"m17-q46","q":"Why must a part-time 4WD not be used in 4H on dry hard roads?","options":["The engine would stall at once","The fuel would be wasted at idle","The axles wind up in turns","The tyres would be too soft"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l10","explain":"With the centre locked, the front and rear shafts turn at the same speed, and in turns the wheels need different speeds, so the driveline is stressed."},
    {"id":"m17-q47","q":"A rear tyre is 3.3% larger in circumference than the front tyres on an AWD car. What happens?","options":["The vehicle has more traction","The coupling slips, making heat","The engine runs more efficiently","Nothing, because the car drives the same"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l10","explain":"The two axles want to turn at different speeds. The centre coupling takes the difference as slip, heat and wear."},
    {"id":"m17-q48","q":"What does the low range of a transfer case do?","options":["It cuts speed to give more torque","It makes the car go much faster","It locks the steering wheel solid","It switches the engine off fully"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l10","explain":"A planetary set multiplies torque, so a vehicle can crawl up steep or soft ground slowly."},
    {"id":"m17-q49","q":"What should be kept the same on an AWD vehicle's four tyres?","options":["The colour of the tyres","Make, model, size and wear","The age of the valves","Nothing, they can differ freely"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l10","explain":"Different circumferences make the system slip. Keep the tyres matched, within the maker's tolerance."},
    {"id":"m17-q50","q":"A gearbox shows slipping and a burnt smell. What should you check first?","options":["The fluid level and condition","The windscreen wipers and the washer bottle level","The air conditioning pressure and the cabin filter","The tyre tread depth and the tyre pressures"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l11","explain":"Fluid problems cause many faults and are simple to check."},
    {"id":"m17-q51","q":"What does a P07xx code usually relate to?","options":["The air conditioning system and its pressure sensors","The fuel system and the injectors on the rail","The transmission system","The ignition system and the coils on each cylinder"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l11","explain":"A code points to a circuit or system, not automatically to a part."},
    {"id":"m17-q52","q":"A delay before drive engages might be caused by what?","options":["A low tyre pressure on one side of the vehicle","A weak horn or a loose bulb in the dashboard","A dirty wiper blade or a low washer fluid level","Low fluid, worn seals or worn clutches"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l11","explain":"Pressure builds up slowly when the fluid is low or seals leak."},
    {"id":"m17-q53","q":"Which job would normally be outside the scope of an ordinary workshop?","options":["Changing the fluid and the filter at the normal service interval","Rebuilding the internal clutches of an automatic gearbox","Replacing a speed sensor on the gearbox housing","Repairing a leaking cooler line near the radiator"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l11","explain":"Many workshops fit a rebuilt or exchange unit."},
    {"id":"m17-q54","q":"What should you do if an automatic gearbox goes into limp mode?","options":["Read the codes and check the fluid and wiring before blaming the gearbox","Clear the codes and ignore the fault until it returns","Replace the whole gearbox at once, without any checks or any codes being read","Add any available fluid to the gearbox and drive on"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l11","explain":"Limp mode protects the gearbox, and the cause is often a sensor or wiring fault."},
    {"id":"m17-q55","q":"Which should be checked first when an automatic gearbox has shift problems?","options":["Paint colour of the bodywork","Tyre pressures only on the car","Fluid level and condition","The radio fuse in the fuse box"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l11","explain":"Many faults are caused by fluid problems. Check it before any further diagnosis, with the correct procedure."},
    {"id":"m17-q56","q":"What does a dark brown, burnt-smelling gearbox fluid show?","options":["Fluid that is brand new","A water leak in the radiator","Overheating and wear","A good, healthy fluid"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m17-l12","explain":"Burnt fluid shows high temperatures, worn friction material and perhaps a failing clutch. Investigate the cause."},
    {"id":"m17-q57","q":"A gearbox holds 8 litres and 3 litres are drained and refilled. How much of the fluid is new?","options":["About 100% of the fluid","About 3% of the fluid","About 8% of the fluid","About 37.5%"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l12","explain":"Only the fluid in the pan is changed. 3 ÷ 8 = 37.5% new. Repeated services renew more."},
    {"id":"m17-q58","q":"Transmission fluid is milky and the coolant has oil in it. What is the likely fault?","options":["A worn tyre on the front axle","A leaking radiator cooler","A weak starter motor on the car","A blocked fuel filter on the line"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m17-l12","explain":"The cooler is in the radiator tank. A leak lets coolant and fluid mix, which destroys a gearbox if it is not fixed."},
    {"id":"m17-q59","q":"How is fluid level checked on a gearbox with a level plug?","options":["With the engine off and the car tilted","With the engine at full speed","With the car on its side","At set temperature, engine running"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m17-l12","explain":"The level is correct when a little fluid trickles out of the plug at the right temperature, as the manual states."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m17-c01","front":"What does a torque converter do?","back":"It connects the engine to the gearbox by fluid and multiplies torque at low speed.","lesson":"m17-l1"},
    {"id":"m17-c02","front":"What does the park pawl do?","back":"It locks the output shaft in park.","lesson":"m17-l1"},
    {"id":"m17-c03","front":"What operates clutches and bands?","back":"Hydraulic pressure controlled by solenoids and the valve body.","lesson":"m17-l1"},
    {"id":"m17-c04","front":"What is a CVT?","back":"A transmission with a belt or chain between variable-width pulleys.","lesson":"m17-l1"},
    {"id":"m17-c05","front":"What is a dual-clutch gearbox?","back":"A gearbox with two clutches that engage odd and even gears.","lesson":"m17-l1"},
    {"id":"m17-c06","front":"What does the stator do?","back":"It multiplies torque at low speed by redirecting fluid.","lesson":"m17-l2"},
    {"id":"m17-c07","front":"What does the lock-up clutch do?","back":"It removes converter slip at cruising speed.","lesson":"m17-l2"},
    {"id":"m17-c08","front":"What is ATF?","back":"Automatic transmission fluid: it transmits force, lubricates and cools.","lesson":"m17-l2"},
    {"id":"m17-c09","front":"What does dark brown, burnt fluid mean?","back":"Overheating and worn clutches.","lesson":"m17-l2"},
    {"id":"m17-c10","front":"What does milky pink fluid mean?","back":"Coolant is leaking into the gearbox.","lesson":"m17-l2"},
    {"id":"m17-c11","front":"What is stall speed?","back":"The maximum engine speed with the vehicle held and the throttle fully open.","lesson":"m17-l2"},
    {"id":"m17-c12","front":"Typical automatic line pressure?","back":"About 3 to 6 bar at idle in drive, and 10 to 15 bar under load.","lesson":"m17-l3"},
    {"id":"m17-c13","front":"What does the TCM compare to find slip?","back":"Input speed against output speed times the gear ratio.","lesson":"m17-l3"},
    {"id":"m17-c14","front":"What does the valve body do?","back":"It routes pressure to clutches and bands using solenoid-driven valves.","lesson":"m17-l3"},
    {"id":"m17-c15","front":"What are the parts of a planetary gear set?","back":"Sun gear, planet carrier and planets, and ring gear.","lesson":"m17-l4"},
    {"id":"m17-c16","front":"How do clutches and bands select a gear?","back":"They hold or drive parts of the planetary gear set.","lesson":"m17-l4"},
    {"id":"m17-c17","front":"What does a slipping automatic suggest?","back":"Low or burnt fluid, worn clutches or low pressure.","lesson":"m17-l4"},
    {"id":"m17-c18","front":"What sets a CVT's ratio?","back":"The ratio of the belt radius on the output pulley to that on the input pulley.","lesson":"m17-l5"},
    {"id":"m17-c19","front":"Fluid for a CVT?","back":"Only the CVT fluid the maker specifies. Never ordinary ATF.","lesson":"m17-l5"},
    {"id":"m17-c20","front":"What is an eCVT?","back":"A hybrid transmission with a planetary gear set linking an engine, generator and motor. It has no belt.","lesson":"m17-l5"},
    {"id":"m17-c21","front":"Ring held, sun input, carrier output?","back":"Ratio = 1 + R ÷ S. With S=30 and R=70 it is 3.33.","lesson":"m17-l6"},
    {"id":"m17-c22","front":"Sun held, ring input, carrier output?","back":"Ratio = 1 + S ÷ R. With S=30 and R=70 it is 1.43.","lesson":"m17-l6"},
    {"id":"m17-c23","front":"Carrier held, sun input, ring output?","back":"Ratio = −R ÷ S. A negative sign is reverse rotation.","lesson":"m17-l6"},
    {"id":"m17-c24","front":"What is a shift logic table?","back":"A table showing which clutches and brakes are applied in each gear. It helps to find a slipping element.","lesson":"m17-l6"},
    {"id":"m17-c25","front":"What do the pinion and crown wheel do?","back":"They turn the drive through 90 degrees and give the final reduction.","lesson":"m17-l7"},
    {"id":"m17-c26","front":"Why is a differential needed?","back":"The outside wheel travels farther, so the wheels must turn at different speeds.","lesson":"m17-l7"},
    {"id":"m17-c27","front":"What is the weakness of an open differential?","back":"A wheel with no grip receives little drive, so the other wheel gets little too.","lesson":"m17-l7"},
    {"id":"m17-c28","front":"What does a limited-slip differential do?","back":"It sends some torque to the wheel with grip.","lesson":"m17-l7"},
    {"id":"m17-c29","front":"What is backlash?","back":"The small clearance between gear teeth.","lesson":"m17-l7"},
    {"id":"m17-c30","front":"Speed rule of an open differential?","back":"The carrier speed is the average of the two wheel speeds.","lesson":"m17-l8"},
    {"id":"m17-c31","front":"Why do open differentials spin one wheel on ice?","back":"Torque is shared equally, so the low-grip wheel limits the torque to both.","lesson":"m17-l8"},
    {"id":"m17-c32","front":"Four types of limited-slip?","back":"Clutch-pack, Torsen, viscous coupling and electronically controlled clutch.","lesson":"m17-l8"},
    {"id":"m17-c33","front":"What is a transfer case?","back":"A gearbox that splits the drive between front and rear axles.","lesson":"m17-l9"},
    {"id":"m17-c34","front":"What is driveline wind-up?","back":"Stress in a part-time 4WD driveline on hard dry roads.","lesson":"m17-l9"},
    {"id":"m17-c35","front":"Why match tyres on AWD?","back":"A size difference makes the centre coupling overheat.","lesson":"m17-l9"},
    {"id":"m17-c36","front":"What is low range for?","back":"Slow, heavy work such as steep hills and towing.","lesson":"m17-l9"},
    {"id":"m17-c37","front":"What causes driveline wind-up?","back":"Locked axles turn at different speeds in a turn and twist against each other. Never use 4H on hard dry roads.","lesson":"m17-l10"},
    {"id":"m17-c38","front":"Why match AWD tyres?","back":"A different circumference makes the coupling slip constantly, causing heat and wear.","lesson":"m17-l10"},
    {"id":"m17-c39","front":"What does P07xx relate to?","back":"Transmission faults.","lesson":"m17-l11"},
    {"id":"m17-c40","front":"What are the first checks for an automatic fault?","back":"Fluid level and condition, codes, wiring and the cooler.","lesson":"m17-l11"},
    {"id":"m17-c41","front":"What is outside normal workshop scope?","back":"Internal rebuilds. Fit a rebuilt or exchange unit.","lesson":"m17-l11"},
    {"id":"m17-c42","front":"How much new fluid after a drain and fill?","back":"The fraction drained over the total. 3 of 8 litres is 37.5%.","lesson":"m17-l12"},
    {"id":"m17-c43","front":"Normal and bad transmission fluid?","back":"Normal is bright red or pink and clean. Dark brown or burnt means overheating. Milky means water.","lesson":"m17-l12"},
    {"id":"m17-c44","front":"How to check the transmission cooler flow?","back":"Disconnect the return line and measure the flow, about a litre in 20 to 30 seconds, as the manual says.","lesson":"m17-l12"}
  ]
});
